{
  "version": 1,
  "name": "Example: Clarke and Park transforms",
  "exampleId": "block-space-phasors",
  "description": "Three sines 120° apart stand for balanced phase currents. The Clarke transform turns them into α = cos ωt and β = sin ωt, a vector turning at 50 Hz. The Park transform rotates that vector by the angle θ = ωt, so in the turning frame it stands still: d = 1 and q = 0. The inverse Clarke transform (with a zero-sequence input of 0) rebuilds the three phases from α and β and drives a controlled three-phase source, the ideal model of an inverter, into a 10 Ω star load: each phase current is the phase voltage over 10 Ω.",
  "duration": 0.04,
  "revision": 0,
